IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mc97-nouns`

-- MAGNA CARTA (1297), LAYER 1: THE TWO PREAMBLE PARAGRAPHS.
-- Source: aep-Edw1cc1929-25-9-extract.md, the heading "Preamble, paragraph 1 (Edward I)" (line 22), its text (line 23),
-- the heading "Preamble, paragraph 2 (Henry III's charter, recited)" (line 25) and its text (line 26).
-- The headings are the extract's own labels. Paragraph 1 is the text's address and recital; paragraph 2 recites a grant.
-- What is encoded: who the text names as speaking at the head of each paragraph, which charter paragraph 1 says it has
-- seen "in these words", and paragraph 2's grant (to whom, where kept, for how long). The address, the greeting and the
-- statement of motive create no rule and are carried in NOTES.md as inert (reason 1).
-- Nothing here says the grant is, or ever was, in force.

§ `Preamble`

§§ `Paragraph 1: who speaks, and the charter it says it has seen`

-- src:23 | EDWARD by the Grace of God King of England, Lord of Ireland, and Duke of Guyan,
-- src:26 | HENRY by the Grace of God King of England, Lord of Ireland, Duke of Normandy and Guyan, and Earl of Anjou,
@ref Magna Carta (1297), preamble paragraphs 1 and 2, the style of the speaker (src:23, src:26)
GIVEN passage IS A Passage
GIVETH A MAYBE Speaker
`the speaker named at the head of` passage MEANS
    CONSIDER passage
        WHEN `preamble paragraph 1` THEN
            JUST (Speaker WITH
                name IS "EDWARD"
                titles IS LIST
                    "King of England"
                    "Lord of Ireland"
                    "Duke of Guyan")
        WHEN `preamble paragraph 2` THEN
            JUST (Speaker WITH
                name IS "HENRY"
                titles IS LIST
                    "King of England"
                    "Lord of Ireland"
                    "Duke of Normandy and Guyan"
                    "Earl of Anjou")
        -- NOTHING, silent: the text names a speaker at the head of two paragraphs only.
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:23 | We have seen the Great Charter of the Lord Henry sometimes King of England, our Father, of the Liberties of England in these words:
@ref Magna Carta (1297), preamble paragraph 1, the recital (src:23)
GIVETH A STRING
`the charter that preamble paragraph 1 says it has seen` MEANS
    "the Great Charter of the Lord Henry sometimes King of England, our Father, of the Liberties of England"

-- gloss (not the text's): "in these words" says that what follows, from paragraph 2 on, is the words of that charter.
-- The text says no more about how the two relate; this encoding makes no claim about it.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), preamble paragraph 1, the recital (src:23)
GIVETH A STRING
`how preamble paragraph 1 introduces what follows` MEANS "in these words"

§§ `Paragraph 2: the grant`

-- The grant in paragraph 2 is made to the classes it lists. The word [Freemen] is bracketed in the text, as an insertion of
-- the traditional translation; the brief for this encoding is to take the text as the site gives it, brackets included.
-- gloss (not the text's): "named" is all the rule says. A class the paragraph does not name (a Knight, say) is not
-- excluded by this paragraph; it is simply not addressed, so the answer for it is "not covered by the text".
-- src:26 | have given and granted to all Archbishops, Bishops, Abbots, Priors, Earls, Barons, and to all [Freemen] of this our Realm, these Liberties following, to be kept in our Kingdom of England for ever.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), preamble paragraph 2, the grant (src:26)
GIVEN capacity IS A Capacity
GIVETH A Coverage
`preamble paragraph 2: these Liberties following are given and granted to` capacity MEANS
    IF      capacity EQUALS `an Archbishop`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Bishop`
         OR capacity EQUALS `an Abbot`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Prior`
         OR capacity EQUALS `an Earl`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Baron`
         OR capacity EQUALS `a Freeman of the Realm`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, unclosed list: the list of classes is not closed with "only", so a class it does not name is not addressed (not "no").
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

-- "to be kept in our Kingdom of England for ever": the place and the time. The text names no one who is to keep them.
-- src:26 | these Liberties following, to be kept in our Kingdom of England for ever.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), preamble paragraph 2, where the Liberties are to be kept (src:26)
GIVETH A STRING
`preamble paragraph 2: where the Liberties following are to be kept` MEANS "our Kingdom of England"

-- src:26 | these Liberties following, to be kept in our Kingdom of England for ever.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), preamble paragraph 2, for how long (src:26)
GIVETH A STRING
`preamble paragraph 2: for how long the Liberties following are to be kept` MEANS "for ever"

-- "to be kept" is a passive with no agent. The text does not say who is to keep the Liberties, so no party is invented:
-- the answer is NOTHING, whoever asks.
-- src:26 | these Liberties following, to be kept in our Kingdom of England for ever.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), preamble paragraph 2, who is to keep them (src:26)
GIVETH A MAYBE Party
`preamble paragraph 2: the party the text names to keep the Liberties following` MEANS NOTHING
